WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.060
Film Courage: Following up on something you
said earlier, you joked about there not being

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.060
a career.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.520
Do you feel like this every time one job ends
because you are essentially…it’s like

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.780
shaking up a snow globe it up [trying to drum
up new work]?

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:12.900
Dar Dixon, Actor: Absolutely!

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.410
Oh my gosh yes.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:25.660
I can’t remember what happened but I was
talking to someone who had the kind of typical

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.520
“Oh you are working in TV and Film?

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.830
Oh my gosh.”

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:35.290
And I’m thinking You know man, I’m on
set, the lights are on, the camera is going,

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:42.100
the mic is hot, the crew is standing around,
they are setting up the shot, changing the

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.230
lens, relighting something (whatever it is).

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.340
There’s a little bit of a break.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:51.210
I’ve got my cell phone out and I’m answering
emails, I’m sending off texts looking for

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.970
work, returning phone calls if I’ve got
a second without interrupting everybody else

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.490
and then put it away and go back into it.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:04.830
Hustling right on the set, in the trailer,
in the makeup chair.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.320
It never ends so I always feel like that.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.000
I think every actor does.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.780
I’ve never heard any actor not saying that
unless you are lucky enough and you get your

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:19.610
own show or you have a proper movie career
where it keeps on coming but even then it’s

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.440
an up and down and you just never know.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.570
Film Courage: That being said, why do you
think people fail at it?

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.570
Dar: Fail at what?

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.610
Film Courage: Fail at having consistent acting
work?

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.840
Dar: That’s a big question.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.780
There are a number of factors at play but
if I kind of pull out and take the meta view

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.580
of it or the 30,000-foot-view.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:50.510
Like if you’re in a plane 35,000-foot whatever
it may be I think people probably aren’t

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.520
clear with what they want.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:02.210
And I also think that most people don’t
investigate themselves and know themselves

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:12.540
well enough and don’t manage their emotional
states better because ultimately everything

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.510
is a psychological game, ultimately right?

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:21.060
Because we’re all out there and everybody
here is talented.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.970
Everyone is talented, ridiculously talented.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.530
The competition level is high.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:32.930
And I played college sports and I’ll never
forget when I got to college and played football

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.260
it was like “Oh my gosh.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.721
These guys are all so talented.

00:02:38.721 --> 00:02:39.721
Wow!”

00:02:39.721 --> 00:02:40.721
Right?

00:02:40.721 --> 00:02:42.069
Then it becomes a mental game, a psychological
game.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:46.910
Managing your emotions, staying focused, making
sure that you are not giving some kind of

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.160
crazy meaning to something that happened that
doesn’t help you, doesn’t serve you and

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:56.900
push you down the field or the path towards
whatever it is you want being clear (your

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.540
goal) and that you notice when you start to
go off course.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:08.180
Maybe a crazy emotional reaction to something
or it gives you a bad feeling or whatever

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.290
right?

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.910
Those things are important and I think that’s
a long-winded answer but that’s why more

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:19.750
people aren’t as successful as they could
be and work more often.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.810
Generally speaking…that is a big question.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.060
But generally speaking I would say to start
there.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.950
Film Courage: I just looked at a book recently
and it was called…I won’t say who the

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.232
person was because I don’t want to sort
of break their anonymity even though there

00:03:32.232 --> 00:03:36.760
is a book out about them but they basically
said it was the female so-and-so.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:44.030
Saying that this person could have become
the male version of a major star if they had

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:49.270
managed their emotions, other things, drinking
was another.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:54.349
So they [the author] showed this person’s
rise from obscurity as the poor relation in

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:59.090
their family to their subsequent downfall
and just how sad this was.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:04.650
I thought it was interesting because it could
be a metaphor for a lot of people and it is

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.019
managing…because crazy things can happen
in Los Angeles.

00:04:09.019 --> 00:04:15.880
And I like what you said about not overacting
or managing emotions and it’s a real skill.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.069
Dar: It is a skill and it’s one that you
have to work at to develop because it’s

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:23.150
not something we are taught.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:29.360
That would be a really, really critical foundational
course that should be in the educational system

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.990
if you’re asking me and I don’t know if
you are but that’s a life skill that is

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:35.990
invaluable.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:46.650
And I’ve got my nerdiness and one of my
things is to study people who have been massively

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:54.560
successful and influential across a broad
spectrum of disciplines and industries.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:04.099
And let me ask you, what do you think would
be the common denominator with the most successful

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:06.479
people (and I know that’s what does successful
mean)?

00:05:06.479 --> 00:05:10.509
So let’s just say financially, spiritually,
emotionally, psychologically, psychically.

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:18.539
The most successful people, what would you
think is is the most common trait that they

00:05:18.539 --> 00:05:19.539
have?

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:23.090
Film Courage: From what I’ve seen, I think
people who are just obsessed with something.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:29.849
It doesn’t even mean that they are good
people or bad people, anywhere on the spectrum

00:05:29.849 --> 00:05:36.409
of intentions but I think being so obsessed
with whatever they are into whether it’s

00:05:36.409 --> 00:05:40.800
acting, whether it’s stock trading, whether
it’s real estate, whether it’s painting,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.219
whether it’s meditation.

00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:46.479
I’ve seen people who’ve just had a laser
focus with something, they are obsessed with

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:48.539
it and it’s their life 7 days a week.

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:56.430
Dar: That is a very common answer and it’s
certainly necessary, the seminal book that

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:00.099
Napoleon Hill wrote Think and Grow Rich “you
have to have that burning desire.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:01.819
White, hot burning desire.”

00:06:01.819 --> 00:06:04.529
Which in today’s world is like “I’m
obsessed.”

00:06:04.529 --> 00:06:06.020
Everybody says obsessed.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:11.789
It’s not necessarily the best word to use
but it’s the word of the day.

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:17.919
So yes that’s it but the trait that people
see (that’s kind of a given), self-compassion.

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:22.220
Film Courage: Wow…so sort of self-awareness
or?

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.259
Dar: Self-compassion.

00:06:23.259 --> 00:06:24.809
Film Courage: Self-compassion.

00:06:24.809 --> 00:06:26.319
Can you take me through what that would be?

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:27.319
Dar: Sure.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.319
Self-empathy.

00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:30.039
Film Courage: So not beating up on one’s
self.

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:31.039
Dar: There you go.

00:06:31.039 --> 00:06:32.840
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.469
I would not have guess that.

00:06:34.469 --> 00:06:36.430
Dar: Neither would I but they all do.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.149
Film Courage: Where did you learn this?

00:06:38.149 --> 00:06:45.800
Dar: From just reading and studying different
people, male/female, different industries,

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:51.050
different disciplines, different races, origins.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:54.210
You are going to make mistakes, you are going
to right?

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:59.419
Since we’re talking about acting and this
is Film Courage and writing.

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:06.979
There’s a lot of writers that watch this
and…I mean what writer do you know that

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:08.179
doesn’t beat themselves up?

00:07:08.179 --> 00:07:10.569
What actor do you know that doesn’t beat
themselves up?

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:11.569
Right?

00:07:11.569 --> 00:07:15.969
Like there’s that neurosis that’s always
sitting on this shoulder here, that anxiety

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:19.259
of here “What are they thinking about me?”

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:22.919
It’s like dude, give yourself a break.

00:07:22.919 --> 00:07:23.969
Relax, right?

00:07:23.969 --> 00:07:27.479
Because reality is most people aren’t even
thinking about you.

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:28.479
Film Courage: That’s true.

00:07:28.479 --> 00:07:30.990
Dar: They are thinking about themselves.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:31.990
P.S.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.931
Yes, they are having the same conversation
in their head that you are.

00:07:34.931 --> 00:07:36.149
Film Courage: And they are thinking that you
are thinking this about them.

00:07:36.149 --> 00:07:38.979
Dar: We’re all human beings.

00:07:38.979 --> 00:07:43.569
Give yourself a break, you are doing the best
you can do with what you have at that time.

00:07:43.569 --> 00:07:50.259
And if you are obsessed as we say or you have
that burning desire (whatever it may be) then

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.780
just trust that you are going to make mistakes
but that’s how you learn.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:55.869
Mistakes are a great thing.

00:07:55.869 --> 00:07:58.749
I never used to think that way.

00:07:58.749 --> 00:07:59.749
I wonder how that sounds?

00:07:59.749 --> 00:08:05.419
It sounds probably crazy to say but mistakes
are a blessing man.

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:06.419
You got problems?

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:07.569
It means you’re still alive.

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:13.629
There’s an old story where a guy and his
son are driving through town and the kid is

00:08:13.629 --> 00:08:15.080
talking about problems, problems, problems.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:16.879
“Dad I just wish I didn’t have any problems.”

00:08:16.879 --> 00:08:19.460
They drive by the graveyard and the dad says
“You know the one place where they don’t

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:20.460
have problems?”

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.289
And he points to the graveyard “Problems
are good.

00:08:22.289 --> 00:08:23.810
They’re a sign of life son.”

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:28.639
Yes, it’s the truth though right?

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.740
You are going to make mistakes, that’s how
you learn.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.840
Pain is the greatest teacher there is.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:39.060
Everyone runs from it to their detriment,
myself included.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.389
Film Courage: I find this very interesting.

00:08:42.389 --> 00:08:47.740
So let’s suppose instead you fail an audition
or someone tells you you’re horrible (whatever)…

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.970
Dar: I’ve never done that so I don’t if
I can relate?

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:55.720
Film Courage: Instead of sort of reenacting
Nicholas Cage’s character in LEAVING LAS

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.030
VEGAS being more like “I’m going to go
for a hike.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:01.820
I’m going to take care of myself.”

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.130
That’s not as sexy and glamorous though.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:11.430
I think a lot of us…Julia Cameron said tortured
artist get way more attention and pats on

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:15.060
the back than healthier ones.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:20.660
I wonder if we fall into almost wanting to
be a tortured artist?

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.130
Because it gets more attention and people
like you more?

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:27.530
Then if everything is great people are going
to be like “Oh, I hate this person.”

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:35.100
Dar: Yes it can definitely happen but it’s
kind of a bull crap narrative it really is.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.520
Film Courage: Tell me.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:42.690
Dar: Well like you just said it’s not as
sexy and glamorous and it’s ironic because

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:46.830
who is glamorized it?

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:50.530
Our industry did.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:58.130
And so the same thing with painters, Van Gough
took his ear off.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:59.130
Nobody knew who he was.

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.470
Everybody thought he was batty crazy and now
he’s worshipped and adored but he’s not

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:10.410
around for 
it anymore.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:11.410
It happens, I understand it.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:12.590
I don’t want to poopoo it.

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:15.260
It’s tough, I’ve wrestled with it and
still do.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:19.150
I’m not immune to it by any stretch of the
imagination.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:24.460
But yes, you’ve got to give yourself a break,
do the best you can and I can’t even believe

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:25.770
I still work.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:31.250
I look back at some of my old work and I just…you
cringe.

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:34.320
I cringe like “Oh my gosh?”

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:35.320
Right?

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:38.631
The imposter syndrome when they say one of
these days they’re going to figure out you

00:10:38.631 --> 00:10:41.110
have know idea what you’re doing.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:42.340
Everyone feels that way.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:52.970
I’ve had the good fortune of knowing, dining
with, being friendly with world leaders and

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:59.390
senators and federal judges and civil rights
leaders and they all have the similar…everyone

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:04.530
across the board, everyone feels like that.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:09.430
I think it would be more helpful if we all
hear that more often and realize that more

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:18.650
often and okay I feel like a fraud and yes…they
can probably see that my eye is twitching

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:22.910
or they can probably hear my stomach gurgling
or I shouldn’t have had that garlic last

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:26.872
night or whatever it may be and you just say
forget it, I’m going to go ahead and do

00:11:26.872 --> 00:11:27.400
it anyway.

